    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "I think it is great that explicitly typed containers benefit from much faster item get and set operations. Naturally.\n\nI would like to propose to specialize these in the same way as was done for comparisons.\nNow, there are ways to get performance without this.\nWith combination of fused types and branches everything is possible.\n\nHowever, things get quite complicated rather quickly as one ventures down the road of implementing optimized methods for generic sequences. Especially when there is more than one sequence in play.\n\nAnd I think for minimal effort - maximum benefit approach this might be worthwhile.\nI think the fact that clear benefit was observed specializing comparisons suggests that such incision shouldn't slow down general case of `(arr: object).__getitem__`, which I believe is slow enough for `type(arr)` and few checks for some base containers to be in the noise.\n\nIn short: specializing `[]` set and get for `str`, `bytes`, `bytearray`, `list`, `tuple` and `dict`.\n\n---\n\nDoes this make sense, in the first place?\nIs this something that is common / desirable enough to make an effort?\n\n---\n\nFor reference - comparison optimizations:\n* https://github.com/cython/cython/issues/7447\n* https://github.com/cython/cython/issues/7474\n\nPotential speedup (for list):\n```python\ndef var_vs_list(mode: Py_ssize_t=0, n: Py_ssize_t=1001):\n    a: list = list(range(10))\n    b: object = list(range(10))\n    v0: Py_ssize_t = 1\n    v1: Py_ssize_t = 10\n    if mode == 1:\n        for _ in range(n):\n            b[0], b[1] = b[1], b[0]\n        return b[0], b[1]\n    elif mode == 2:\n        for _ in range(n):\n            a[0], a[1] = a[1], a[0]\n        return a[0], a[1]\n\n%timeit var_vs_list(1)     # 18000 (from this)\n%timeit var_vs_list(2)     #  5000 (to this)\n```",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "My feeling is that most of the obvious stuff is already done.\n\nThe index itself is almost always an int or a slice. But that's also almost always known at compile time.\n\nAt least for the `int` indexing there's fast paths for list and tuple.\n\nFor string types, I think there's only an advantage if you know the expected return type is a character type. That's potentially an optimisation that we don't do: if we know that we're expecting a character but don't know the type being indexed then maybe we should have fast paths for strings.",
